use std
into spice.amni
dialect js

This file is a library built for the web as a wasm module; its `main()` does nothing. JavaScript drives it through its exported functions (every name starting with sp_ is exported). It is a SPICE-style circuit simulator: modified nodal analysis with Newton iteration for the operating point, DC sweeps, small-signal AC sweeps and transient runs. JavaScript parses the netlist into numeric tables; this module does all the math. Its only import line is `import math;`, ending with a semicolon. Every int used in real math is converted with `as real`, and a real used as an int with `as int`, since Helium has no real or int conversion calls.

```amni
extern fn sp_view_f64(id i32, xs real[:]) = "sp_view_f64";
extern fn sp_view_i32(id i32, xs i32[:]) = "sp_view_i32";
```

Circuit layout. Nodes are numbered 0 (ground) to `NN`; branches (extra unknown currents) are numbered 0 to `NB` minus 1. The unknown vector has `NV` entries, `NN` plus `NB`: matrix index n minus 1 is the voltage of node n (ground has none), and matrix index `NN` plus b is the current of branch b. Element e has 8 ints in `ET` starting at 8e: 0 type, 1 to 4 nodes n1 to n4, 5 own branch, 6 controlling branch, 7 kind; and 16 reals in `EV` starting at 16e, and 8 reals of state in `SV` starting at 8e. Types: 1 resistor (EV 0 resistance), 2 capacitor (EV 0 capacitance), 3 inductor (EV 0 inductance, own branch), 4 voltage source (own branch), 5 current source, 6 diode (n1 anode, n2 cathode; EV 0 saturation current Is, 1 emission coefficient N), 7 bipolar transistor (n1 collector, n2 base, n3 emitter; kind 1 NPN or minus 1 PNP; EV 0 Is, 1 forward beta Bf, 2 reverse beta Br, 3 Early voltage Vaf where 0.0 means none), 8 MOSFET (n1 drain, n2 gate, n3 source; kind 1 NMOS or minus 1 PMOS; EV 0 beta in amps per volt squared, 1 threshold Vto, 2 lambda), 9 voltage-controlled voltage source (own branch; n3, n4 control; EV 0 gain), 10 voltage-controlled current source (n3, n4 control; EV 0 transconductance), 11 current-controlled current source (controlling branch; EV 0 gain), 12 current-controlled voltage source (own branch and controlling branch; EV 0 transresistance). For sources (types 4 and 5) EV 0 is the DC value, 1 the AC magnitude, 2 the AC phase in degrees, and kind is the waveform: 0 constant, 1 sine with EV 3 to 7 offset, amplitude, frequency, delay and damping, 2 pulse with EV 3 to 9 initial value, pulsed value, delay, rise time, fall time, pulse width and period, 3 piecewise linear with EV 3 the start index and EV 4 the pair count of (time, value) pairs in `PW`.

Module state. Ints, all starting at 0: `NN`, `NB`, `NV`, `NE` (element count), `LIM` (1 when the last stamp limited a junction), `ITS` (total Newton iterations). Reals: `GM` (gmin, starting at 1e-12), `SC` (source scale, starting at 1.0). Growable f64 arrays: `EV`, `SV`, `PW` (piecewise-linear pairs), `FQ` (AC frequencies in hertz), `PR` (8 parameters), `ST` (8 statistics), `AR` and `AI` (the real and imaginary matrix, `NV` times `NV`, row-major), `BR` and `BI` (right-hand side), `XR` and `XI` (solution), `XS` and `SS` (saved copies of `XR` and `SV`), `OUT` (result rows). Growable i32 array: `ET`. Parameters in `PR`: 0 relative tolerance (default 1e-3), 1 voltage tolerance (1e-6), 2 current tolerance (1e-12), 3 thermal voltage (0.025865), 4 Newton iteration limit (100). Statistics in `ST`: 0 status (0 fine, 1 no convergence, 2 singular matrix), 1 total Newton iterations, 2 result row count, 3 result row width.

Sizing helpers. `zf(xs, n, x)` takes an f64 growable array by reference: xs is emptied, then gets `reserve`(n), then is resized to n entries equal to x. `zi(xs, n, x)` does the same for an i32 growable array, resizing to n entries equal to x as i32. Every array in this file is sized only through these two helpers.

The exported function `sp_views()`, declared with the export keyword, registers every array JavaScript reads or writes through sp_view_f64 and sp_view_i32: f64 views 1 `EV`, 2 `PW`, 3 `FQ`, 4 `PR`, 5 `ST`, 6 `OUT`, 7 `XR`, 8 `SV`; i32 view 9 `ET`.

The exported function `sp_setup(nn, nb, ne, npw, nfq)`, declared with the export keyword, takes five i32 counts: nodes, branches, elements, piecewise-linear pairs and AC frequencies. It sets `NN`, `NB`, `NE` and `NV` (nn plus nb), sizes `ET` to 8 times ne zeros, `EV` to 16 times ne zeros, `SV` and `SS` to 8 times ne zeros, `PW` to 2 times npw plus 2 zeros, `FQ` to nfq plus 1 zeros, `AR` and `AI` to `NV` times `NV` zeros, `BR`, `BI`, `XR`, `XI` and `XS` to `NV` zeros, `OUT` to 1 zero, `ST` to 8 zeros and `PR` to the 8 values 1e-3, 1e-6, 1e-12, 0.025865, 100.0, 0.0, 0.0, 0.0, then calls `sp_views` and returns `NV`.

The exported function `sp_init()`, declared with the export keyword, runs after JavaScript filled `ET`, `EV`, `PW` and `PR`: it zeroes `XR`, `XI` and `SV`, sets `GM` to 1e-12, `SC` to 1.0 and `ITS` to 0, then gives every diode SV[8e] equal to 0.6 and every bipolar transistor SV[8e] equal to 0.6 (base-emitter) and SV[8e plus 1] equal to 0.0, and returns 0.

`aa(r, c, v, im)` takes i32 matrix indices r and c, a real v and an i32 im: when r and c are both at least 0 it adds v to entry r times `NV` plus c of `AR` when im is 0, or of `AI` otherwise.

`nd(n)` takes an i32 node and returns n minus 1, its matrix index (minus 1 for ground).

`vc(a, b, c, d, g, im)` stamps a current g times (voltage of node c minus voltage of node d) that leaves node a and enters node b: aa(nd(a), nd(c), g, im), aa(nd(a), nd(d), minus g, im), aa(nd(b), nd(c), minus g, im), aa(nd(b), nd(d), g, im). With a equal to c and b equal to d it is a plain conductance.

`cs(a, b, i, im)` stamps a current i that flows from node a through the element to node b: when nd(a) is at least 0, entry nd(a) of `BR` (im 0) or `BI` (im not 0) loses i; when nd(b) is at least 0, entry nd(b) gains i.

`vx(n)` returns the voltage of node n from `XR`: 0.0 for ground, else XR[n minus 1].

`pnj(vn, vo, vt, is)` is the SPICE junction voltage limiter, all reals: vcrit is vt times the natural log of vt divided by (the square root of 2 times is); when vn is above vcrit and the absolute value of vn minus vo is above 2 times vt, then when vo is above 0.0 the argument a is 1.0 plus (vn minus vo) divided by vt, and the result is vo plus vt times the natural log of a when a is above 0.0 or vcrit otherwise, and when vo is not above 0.0 the result is vt times the natural log of vn divided by vt; it sets `LIM` to 1 whenever it changes the value; otherwise it returns vn.

`ex(x)` returns the exponential of the smaller of x and 80.0.

`wave(e, t)` returns the value of source element e at time t from its `EV` (base 16e) and kind ET[8e plus 7]: kind 0 gives EV 0; kind 1 gives offset when t is below the delay, else offset plus amplitude times the exponential of minus damping times (t minus delay) times the sine of 2 pi times frequency times (t minus delay); kind 2 gives the initial value when t is below the delay, else with tt equal to t minus delay, reduced modulo the period when the period is above 0.0, rise equal to the larger of the rise time and 1e-12 and fall equal to the larger of the fall time and 1e-12: when tt is below rise, initial plus (pulsed minus initial) times tt divided by rise; when tt is below rise plus width, the pulsed value; when tt is below rise plus width plus fall, pulsed plus (initial minus pulsed) times (tt minus rise minus width) divided by fall; else the initial value; kind 3 reads m pairs starting at pair index s of `PW` (time at PW[2(s plus k)], value at PW[2(s plus k) plus 1]) and gives the first value when t is at or before the first time, the last value when t is at or after the last time, and otherwise the linear interpolation between the two pairs around t.

`stamp(mode, t, h, be, w)` builds the matrix for one Newton step from the current `XR`: mode 0 is DC (operating point), 1 is a transient step ending at time t with step h (be 1 for backward Euler, 0 for trapezoidal), 2 is AC at angular frequency w. It zeroes `AR`, `AI`, `BR` and `BI`, sets `LIM` to 0, and adds `GM` to the diagonal of `AR` for every node row (0 up to `NN` minus 1). Then for each element e with t1 to t4 its nodes ET[8e plus 1] to ET[8e plus 4], r equal to `NN` plus its own branch ET[8e plus 5], q equal to `NN` plus its controlling branch ET[8e plus 6], p its kind as a real, the reals v0 to v9 equal to EV[16e] to EV[16e plus 9], and vt equal to PR[3], by type:
- Resistor: vc(t1, t2, t1, t2, 1.0 divided by v0, 0).
- Capacitor: in mode 1 with u the voltage of t1 minus the voltage of t2 at the last accepted step SV[8e plus 2] and i the last current SV[8e plus 3]: g is v0 divided by h when be is 1, else 2 times v0 divided by h; vc(t1, t2, t1, t2, g, 0); cs(t1, t2, minus g times u, 0) when be is 1, else cs(t1, t2, minus (g times u plus i), 0). In mode 2, vc(t1, t2, t1, t2, w times v0, 1). Nothing in mode 0.
- Inductor: aa(nd(t1), r, 1.0, 0), aa(nd(t2), r, minus 1.0, 0), aa(r, nd(t1), 1.0, 0), aa(r, nd(t2), minus 1.0, 0); in mode 1 with u equal to SV[8e plus 2] and i equal to SV[8e plus 3]: when be is 1, aa(r, r, minus v0 divided by h, 0) and BR[r] equal to minus v0 divided by h times i; else aa(r, r, minus 2 times v0 divided by h, 0) and BR[r] equal to minus 2 times v0 divided by h times i minus u. In mode 2, aa(r, r, minus w times v0, 1).
- Voltage source: the same four incidence entries as the inductor; BR[r] is `SC` times v0 in mode 0, wave(e, t) in mode 1; in mode 2 BR[r] is v1 times the cosine of v2 in radians and BI[r] is v1 times the sine of v2 in radians.
- Current source: cs(t1, t2, `SC` times v0, 0) in mode 0, cs(t1, t2, wave(e, t), 0) in mode 1; in mode 2, cs(t1, t2, v1 times the cosine of v2 in radians, 0) and cs(t1, t2, v1 times the sine of v2 in radians, 1).
- Diode: nv is v1 times vt (v1 at least 1e-3); when mode is not 2, the new junction voltage is pnj(vx(t1) minus vx(t2), SV[8e], nv, v0) and is stored in SV[8e]; vd is SV[8e]; ed is ex(vd divided by nv); id is v0 times (ed minus 1.0); gd is v0 divided by nv times ed plus `GM`; vc(t1, t2, t1, t2, gd, 0); when mode is not 2, cs(t1, t2, id minus gd times vd, 0).
- Bipolar transistor: c, b, x are t1, t2, t3; when mode is not 2, SV[8e] becomes pnj(p times (vx(b) minus vx(x)), SV[8e], vt, v0) and SV[8e plus 1] becomes pnj(p times (vx(b) minus vx(c)), SV[8e plus 1], vt, v0); vbe is SV[8e] and vbc is SV[8e plus 1]; eb is ex(vbe divided by vt), ec is ex(vbc divided by vt); fi is v0 times (eb minus 1.0), ri is v0 times (ec minus 1.0), gf is v0 divided by vt times eb plus `GM`, gr is v0 divided by vt times ec plus `GM`; kq is 1.0 minus vbc divided by v3 when v3 is above 0.0, else 1.0; it is (fi minus ri) times kq; gmf is gf times kq; gmr is minus gr times kq minus (fi minus ri) divided by v3 when v3 is above 0.0, else minus gr times kq; vc(c, x, b, x, gmf, 0), vc(c, x, b, c, gmr, 0), vc(b, x, b, x, gf divided by v1, 0), vc(b, c, b, c, gr divided by v2, 0); when mode is not 2, cs(c, x, p times (it minus gmf times vbe minus gmr times vbc), 0), cs(b, x, p times (fi divided by v1 minus gf divided by v1 times vbe), 0) and cs(b, c, p times (ri divided by v2 minus gr divided by v2 times vbc), 0).
- MOSFET: when mode is not 2, the raw vgs is p times (vx(t2) minus vx(t3)) and vds is p times (vx(t1) minus vx(t3)); when vds is below 0.0 the drain and source swap: d is t3, s is t1, vgs becomes p times (vx(t2) minus vx(t1)) and vds becomes minus vds; otherwise d is t1 and s is t3; with lm0 equal to the larger of 0.5 and the absolute value of SV[8e] minus v1, vgs is clamped to SV[8e] minus lm0 up to SV[8e] plus lm0, and `LIM` is set to 1 when the clamp changes vgs; SV[8e] gets vgs, SV[8e plus 1] gets vds and SV[8e plus 2] gets 1.0 when swapped or 0.0 when not. In mode 2 it reads vgs, vds and the swap flag from SV and picks d and s the same way. With vov equal to vgs minus v1 and lm equal to 1.0 plus v2 times vds: when vov is not above 0.0, ids, gm and gds are 0.0; when vds is below vov, ids is v0 times (vov times vds minus vds squared divided by 2) times lm, gm is v0 times vds times lm, gds is v0 times (vov minus vds) times lm plus v0 times (vov times vds minus vds squared divided by 2) times v2; otherwise ids is v0 divided by 2 times vov squared times lm, gm is v0 times vov times lm and gds is v0 divided by 2 times vov squared times v2. Then vc(d, s, t2, s, gm, 0), vc(d, s, d, s, gds plus `GM`, 0), and when mode is not 2, cs(d, s, p times (ids minus gm times vgs minus gds times vds), 0).
- Voltage-controlled voltage source: aa(nd(t1), r, 1.0, 0), aa(nd(t2), r, minus 1.0, 0), aa(r, nd(t1), 1.0, 0), aa(r, nd(t2), minus 1.0, 0), aa(r, nd(t3), minus v0, 0), aa(r, nd(t4), v0, 0).
- Voltage-controlled current source: vc(t1, t2, t3, t4, v0, 0).
- Current-controlled current source: aa(nd(t1), q, v0, 0) and aa(nd(t2), q, minus v0, 0).
- Current-controlled voltage source: aa(nd(t1), r, 1.0, 0), aa(nd(t2), r, minus 1.0, 0), aa(r, nd(t1), 1.0, 0), aa(r, nd(t2), minus 1.0, 0), aa(r, q, minus v0, 0).

`csolve(cx)` solves (`AR` plus i `AI`) x equal to (`BR` plus i `BI`) by Gaussian elimination with partial pivoting on the squared magnitude, working in place on the four arrays, and writes the answer to `XR` and `XI`; when cx is 0 it uses only `AR` and `BR` (real arithmetic, `XI` zeroed). For each column k it picks the row at or below k with the largest pivot magnitude, returns 0 when that squared magnitude is below 1e-300, swaps the rows (matrix and right-hand side), and eliminates below; then it back-substitutes. It returns 1 on success.

`newton(mode, t, h, be)` iterates up to PR[4] as int times: copy `XR` into `XS`, stamp(mode, t, h, be, 0.0), csolve(0); when csolve returns 0 it sets ST[0] to 2.0, restores `XR` from `XS` and returns minus 1; `ITS` grows by 1; converged means every entry i has the absolute value of XR[i] minus XS[i] at most PR[0] times the larger of the absolute values of XR[i] and XS[i] plus the tolerance, which is PR[1] for i below `NN` and PR[2] otherwise; when converged, `LIM` is 0 and this is not the first iteration it returns the iteration count. After the limit it sets ST[0] to 1.0 and returns minus 1.

`opx()` finds the DC operating point: when newton(0, 0.0, 1.0, 0) is at least 0 it returns 1. Otherwise gmin stepping: `GM` starts at 1e-3 and while `GM` is at least 1e-12 it runs newton(0, 0.0, 1.0, 0) and stops the stepping on failure, else divides `GM` by 10; `GM` is then set back to 1e-12, and when the stepping reached the end and a final newton(0, 0.0, 1.0, 0) succeeds it returns 1. Otherwise source stepping: `XR` is zeroed, and for s from 1 to 10 `SC` is s divided by 10.0 and newton(0, 0.0, 1.0, 0) runs; on a failure `SC` goes back to 1.0 and it returns 0. After all ten it sets `SC` to 1.0 and returns 1. On success ST[0] is 0.0.

`row(j, x, cx)` writes result row j of `OUT`, whose rows are ST[3] as int wide: the first entry is x, then the `NV` entries of `XR`, then when cx is 1 the `NV` entries of `XI`.

`size(rows, w)` sets ST[2] to rows and ST[3] to w as reals and sizes `OUT` with `zf` to rows times w zeros.

The exported function `sp_op()`, declared with the export keyword, calls size(1, 1 plus `NV`), runs `opx`, writes row(0, 0.0, 0) when it succeeds, sets ST[1] to `ITS`, calls `sp_views`, and returns 1 on success or 0 on failure.

The exported function `sp_dc(e, a, b, st)`, declared with the export keyword, sweeps the DC value EV[16e] of element e from real a to real b in steps st: n is the floor of (b minus a) divided by st plus 1e-9, plus 1, at least 1; it calls size(n, 1 plus `NV`), keeps the old EV[16e], and for each j from 0 up to n minus 1 sets EV[16e] to a plus j times st, runs `opx` (each point starts from the previous answer) and on success writes row(j, EV[16e], 0); on a failure it sets ST[2] to j, restores EV[16e] and stops. It restores EV[16e], sets ST[1] to `ITS`, calls `sp_views` and returns ST[2] as int.

The exported function `sp_ac(nf)`, declared with the export keyword, runs after `sp_op`: it calls size(nf, 1 plus 2 times `NV`), keeps a copy of `XR` in `XS`, calls stamp(0, 0.0, 1.0, 0, 0.0) once so every device state in `SV` matches the converged node voltages (the operating point solve ends with states from the iterate before it), restores `XR` from `XS`, and for each j from 0 up to nf minus 1 with w equal to 2 pi times FQ[j]: stamp(2, 0.0, 1.0, 0, w), csolve(1), and when it fails sets ST[0] to 2.0 and stops, else writes row(j, FQ[j], 1). Then `XR` gets back the copy from `XS`; it calls `sp_views` and returns ST[0] as int.

The exported function `sp_tran(ts, tf)`, declared with the export keyword, takes the real output step ts and stop time tf. n is the round of tf divided by ts, at least 1; it calls size(n plus 1, 1 plus `NV`). It runs `opx`; on failure it calls `sp_views` and returns minus 1. Then every capacitor gets SV[8e plus 2] equal to the voltage of its n1 minus its n2 and SV[8e plus 3] equal to 0.0, and every inductor SV[8e plus 2] equal to 0.0 and SV[8e plus 3] equal to XR at `NN` plus its own branch; it writes row(0, 0.0, 0). For each step k from 1 to n: the interval starts at t0 equal to (k minus 1) times ts; sub starts at 1; it copies `SV` into `SS` and `XR` into the last-good vector (an extra local copy); then repeatedly, for each substep s from 1 to sub with hh equal to ts divided by sub and tn equal to t0 plus s times hh, be equal to 1 when k is 1 and s is 1 and 0 otherwise: newton(1, tn, hh, be); on failure the try fails; on success every capacitor updates its state, with u equal to the voltage of n1 minus n2 and g as in the stamp: the new current is g times (u minus SV[8e plus 2]) when be is 1, else g times (u minus SV[8e plus 2]) minus SV[8e plus 3], then SV[8e plus 2] gets u and SV[8e plus 3] the new current; every inductor sets SV[8e plus 2] to the voltage of n1 minus n2 and SV[8e plus 3] to XR at `NN` plus its own branch. When the try succeeds it moves on; when it fails `SV` is restored from `SS`, `XR` from the last-good copy, sub is multiplied by 4, and when sub is above 4096 it sets ST[2] to k, ST[1] to `ITS`, calls `sp_views` and returns minus 1. After the interval it writes row(k, k times ts, 0). At the end ST[0] is 0.0, ST[1] is `ITS`, it calls `sp_views` and returns n plus 1.

`main()` does nothing.
